{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794861633,"nonce":"mE8QUNzWxtiMnKngehuN0sAngmw=","message":"More from hxn (2026-11-16, with the local build d41f6463 and the real core count, HXN_PAGE_CORES=3 and =2): with await widened to Ash, both GPU drivers pass at the real core count and at 3 cores, no OOM. At HXN_PAGE_CORES=2, one thread agent, both fail after the await: hxn-gpu-webgpu 'out of memory allocating an array', hxn-gpu-webgl2 'page did not finish in 120 s'. The released drivers, which do not await under Ash, fail at 2 cores too (OOM on 0.1.20, timeout on the local build), so it is not the await or d41f6463. At that point the page starts no Rust thread under Ash: hxn's Inbox starts one Haxe thread for deliveries and the drivers use ash_host_watch. So with one agent something already breaks, and the guess about a native thread on the main scheduler does not cover it. GitHub's macOS runners have 3 cores, which pass. Reproducer for the 2-core case: hxn-gpu-webgpu at its current main, HXN_BROWSER_ARGS=--enable-unsafe-webgpu HXN_PAGE_CORES=2 hxn check hl --wasm --threads --browser with hxn 0.3.6. Not looked at yet here. Next: reproduce it with a Haxe program that starts one thread and waits, at 2 cores, to see whether it is the thread, the one agent, or something the drivers do.","files":null}]}